00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
For this short session,
we get to enjoy something a little

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
fun and a little lighthearted.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll be welcoming Raphaël Semeteys, who
will be presenting, among other things,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
Some tips for making our QR codes look
better, especially the ones where you

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can leave feedback or other
comments. So it'll be fun.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Raphaël is a Developer Relations
Specialist at Worldline.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
He's an architect; he
does all kinds of things.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
He's even a yoga instructor.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
So I'll let you enjoy his little
rant about QR codes

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
and enter his world.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'll hand it over to you,
Raphaël. Thank you, thank you.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Hello, everyone. Thank you for
sticking with me this long.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's the very end of DevFest Nantes.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Right before the closing keynote,
we're going to talk - thanks

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Ambre for the intro -

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
about QR codes and what we can
do to make them look better

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
in the age of GenAI.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
You introduced me—no need to say
anything else except that I'm a geek.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Yes, I co-host a podcast called
"Projets libres" about open source,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
in French. Go
check it out—it's great.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
What I suggest is that we
start by just reviewing

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.891 line:95% align:center position:50%
a couple of quick basics
about QR codes to help you

00:01:16.091 --> 00:01:17.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
understand how it works.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then we'll play
around with GenAI on that.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, QR codes were developed in 1994

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
It was developed in Japan by this man,
Masahiro Hara, at Toyota,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
and became an ISO standard in 2000

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
and we can see that it's still
going strong, since last year,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
there had still been an
update to the spec.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
It is said to be inspired
by the game of Go.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, it's a 2D version of the
barcodes we were already familiar with

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
with black squares on a white
background or vice versa—it depends

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
how we see things.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
It allows you to store digital content,
alphanumeric characters,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
binary data, or kanji.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
We can see the Japanese origins of this.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
They come in several sizes, which are
defined in the specification or "versions",

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
that's the official name in the spec
for V1, which is 21 squares by 21,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Up to the V40, it's 177.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And in fact, each version is four
times larger than the previous one.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are also a number of
things that are in the spec.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not going to go into all the details.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's one thing that's important:
it's a built-in mechanism

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
error correction.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what makes it possible to
scan QR codes, even when they're

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
partially illegible or damaged.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
The Anatomy of a QR Code.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are therefore certain requirements
that are defined and

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
mandatory for

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
QR code readers can scan the code.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are elements that
make it easy to read.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, we see this concept of
a "white zone" that will

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:46.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
outline the QR code and the position
markers—also known as the "eyes."

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, those are the three
squares you'll find.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that will allow a scanner to
recognize: "This is a QR code."

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I can definitely
recognize the QR code.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
There is also an alignment pattern, a

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
or more than one,
which can be placed within the QR

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
code—it's the little eye, if we

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
means right there,
in the lower right corner.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that helps with orientation,
since sometimes we're not always

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
directly in front of the QR code,
so that's how you handle it.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
Depending on the version—and there
are several of them—but in this case,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm on version 3, 29 out of 29,
so right now, I only need one.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
And all of that is
defined in the standard.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
And so the content is
stored in binary format.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
A black square is worth 1,
and a white square is worth 0.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
The dotted lines you see
here help to set

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
the size of a module.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
A square is called a "module" in the spec.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
So when the scanner looks at this,
it will determine the size of the module.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, we have metadata.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what I highlighted in blue.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll store things in designated
areas within the QR code

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
such as the format and, consequently,
the level of error correction used.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:53.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
We'll talk about that.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:58.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm not going to talk about the
masking patterns or the version itself.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, starting from there, beginning at
the bottom, right there on the right,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
By moving up in a zigzag pattern,
you can start storing the contents.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
So here, I've put a white
square, so that's a zero.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:10.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then there's one.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
And now I'm zigzagging my way back up,
one-one, one-one, one-zero.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:15.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, we fill in all the gray areas
like this, then we go back down,

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:20.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then we're done, and we head back
up. That's how a QR code works.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
The error-correction
mechanism—that's what interests me.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:30.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
That, as I said, makes it possible
to always display the QR code

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
usable, even when it is partially damaged.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is done by adding additional
data that will compensate for it

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
any missing data.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
That will add rows and columns,
so it will increase the size, so

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
the QR code version, possibly.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
The implementation is the
Reed-Solomon algorithm.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's common knowledge.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'll spare you the details
of polynomial algebra

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
and finite bodies,
but these are mechanisms that are

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
used for DVD storage, for one thing,
or in RAID 6 configurations,

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
on disk security, as well
as on network communications

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
such as DSL or certain types
of satellite communications.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it's not something completely new.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
since QR codes aren't
exactly a brand-new concept either.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Basically, it works with 8-bit
codewords that are either codewords of

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
data, or correction codewords.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, they will be stored
using the mechanisms I described.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are four possible
levels of correction.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it ranges from low to high.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
And depending on the level,
it ranges from about 7% to 30% of the QR

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
code that may still be unreadable.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
But with this error-correction
mechanism, we'll be able to

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
reconstruct the data.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:40.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
And that's what has historically
been used to customize QR codes.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because, actually, I put my
company's logo there, so I'm working

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
At Worldline, yes, we know that.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
It was placed there,
in the middle of the QR code.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
And actually, the scanners will
interpret that as an error.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it needs to fall between
7% and 30%, depending on

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
of the QR code version.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's the trick—it's a hack that
lets you always scan the QR code

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
code by inserting an image into it. Okay?

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
So that's the standard way,
so to speak, of generating QR codes.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
So now, what could be more fun than that?

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because this time, I changed
the color and then added a logo.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
What could be more fun than
that in the age of image generation

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
and therefore AI?

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Part Two.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
What I'm going to do is...

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:32.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
For those who know me, I come from
the world of open source, so to speak

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
and I really like doing things locally.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
For me, my use case was: I want
to do all of this, run the models,

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:40.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
run everything on this PC.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's another important thing, too.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
What I did was decide to use

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:47.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Stable Diffusion,
which is a family of image

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
generation models.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
I chose a version that isn't
very recent because, for my

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:54.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
use case it's enough.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:58.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's a smaller model than the more
recent ones, such as Flux, etc.,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
which can also be run locally.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
How does Stable Diffusion work?

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
It performs progressive
noise reduction on an image.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
It takes a noisy image or an
image that I provide as input

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
and by working in steps,
it will progressively remove the noise.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
He reduces the noise by adding
elements to the image that will

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
follow the prompts.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is the text where I'll describe
what I want to appear in the image

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:25.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
and possibly an image,
if I provide one as input.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is a family of models
that are referred to as "open,"

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
in the sense that we can retrieve
the weights and thus run the model

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
locally, and possibly adapt it, fine-tune
it, and create derivative versions of it.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
There is a de facto community centered
around this openness that

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
is quite dynamic.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are quite a few components available,
including ComfyUI, which is a front-end

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
or a GUI that's also installed locally,
which will run right

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
there on my PC, and

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
as we can see, it allows
you to create workflows.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is the image
positioned a the bottom.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
So it's completely no-code.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can't tell it's written
in Python—everything's fine.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because there's a lot going
on behind those knots.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're going to run some models,
we're going to run...

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:12.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
You'll see some things that we're
going to add to our workflows.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
But it's very modular.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this case, it really
is open source—GPL v3.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
That openness means there's
a community that contributes

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
a lot of extensions.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
They're called "custom nodes" in ComfyUI,
and they have the advantage of

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
to also run on Windows, Mac, and Linux.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:34.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
For ControlNets, what I need is,

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
I need to have a technique
because what I'm going to do is,

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's just that I'm going to use a
ControlNet that I've already

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:40.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
generated, because for generating

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
ControlNets, there are
plenty of libraries that do that,

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are plenty of
websites that do that, etc.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
So I didn't include that in
my workflow—I could have,

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:47.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
But that's not necessary.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
I want to have a ControlNet as input to modify it using my workflows

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
to turn it into something else.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
So what did I say !?

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
ControlNet ? I meant QR code.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
And what I'm going to use for
that is a technique called

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
ControlNet—that's why I slipped up.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what is ControlNet?

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
These are additional components
that I'll be able to add to my

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
workflows, and these correspond to
additional AI models that enable

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:17.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
to add an additional check
to the generation process.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:21.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
What I need to explain a
little is this latent space that you

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
may have seen
in a previous slide.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
In fact, Stable Diffusion—and
that's the original innovation

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
of Stable Diffusion
is that it doesn't work

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
on the pixels and on the image itself;
it works on a compressed version

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
and represented in a
multidimensional vector space,

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:40.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
kind of like LLMs, in general, with
the concept of embeddings and all that,

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're on the same concepts.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's where all that denoising process I
told you about will take place, and so on.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
And once the image has been captured,
it will then be encoded

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
in pixels and displayed.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
And ControlNets—they allow
you to influence this

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
the mechanism that takes place in that
latent space by extracting only certain

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
characteristics of an image
that I could provide as input.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
And what I want here is
a specialized ControlNet

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
on: I recognize that this is a QR code,
and I'm going to retrieve these

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
features to make sure that
when you create a new image,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
you transfer the
characteristics of the QR code.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
I use a specialized ControlNet
called QR Code Monster,

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
whose purpose is precisely to do that
and ensure that it remains readable,

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Of course, because otherwise,
it's not very useful.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:27.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what I'm suggesting
is that we give it a try.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
What I did was record,

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
and I assure you that everything
was done on that PC, but I recorded my

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
executions—because I want to show you
several examples, so I sped them up

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.297 line:95% align:center position:50%
because otherwise, it just doesn't

00:10:38.497 --> 00:10:39.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
hold together at all in 20 minutes.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay? From here on, I'll show you this

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:46.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if you can see much, but in
any case, you can see that there are some

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:48.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
boxes that are linked to one another.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:50.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
The green box is the prompt.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:55.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Basically, what I'm saying is that I
want a rural landscape with the sky

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
blue, flowers, etc.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:59.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
The red box represents the negative
prompt—what I don't want

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:00.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
to see in the image.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it allows for more
precise control over things.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
The blue section at the bottom
contains the items that are

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:06.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
related to my ControlNet.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, I gave my
QR code as an input.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
And here, in the boxes below,
I'll choose the version

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:13.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
the ControlNet I'm going to use.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:15.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is the version for
Stable Diffusion 1.5,

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
since that's the model I loaded.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
I can configure a number of things here.

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, when it comes to the KSampler,
that's really where all the action happens

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
mathematics, matrix
operations in latent space.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I'm going to do it in 50 steps.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:33.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Guidance refers to the impact
of the prompt on the generation.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:36.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
And at that point, I didn't speed it up.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's so you can see what's on my
PC—this PC has an NVIDIA

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:40.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
4070 graphics card, and it

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
has only 8 GB of VRAM, which is actually
pretty low for this kind of thing.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
So here, you can see how
long it takes to generate.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:50.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
See, there's the seed right there.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if you can read this,
but that's the random part

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
which is used to create the random image

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
which is used
at the beginning for denoising.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:00.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay?

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
As you can see, I get this kind of result.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:05.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, what was I able to achieve?

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:07.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
With my workflow, I was able to...

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:11.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
You saw it in the previous workflow; here,
I have a few boxes,

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's pretty simple in that regard,
but I already have

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:18.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
a ControlNet at the bottom, but you see,
the more boxes I add, the more I have

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:19.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
parameters I can adjust.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:24.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
So that gives you more precise control,
but at the same time, it can be a little...

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:27.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's easy to get lost
with all these settings.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
It takes a little fine-tuning to find
the right workflow, but once you do,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:32.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Once you have the workflow,
you're all set.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, we're capable of doing that.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
If I fix the seed—that is,
if I say, “I don’t want

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
random elements in my workflow".

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:41.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's one thing you need to
understand about Stable Diffusion:

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
the same
settings on the same workflow,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
with the same values, will always
generate the same image—every time.

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:50.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's math—there's no
magic involved, really.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's math—it involves matrix
operations performed in latent space.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:57.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, the guys who made ComfyUI
are really smart because they

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:59.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
said: "We're going to put all of
these elements into the metadata."

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:00.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Image EXIF data.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
That means an image that
was generated using ComfyUI,

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:06.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
I just drag and drop on the ComfyUI
interface, and boom—I've got my workflow

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
that made it possible
to generate it. Awesome.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:09.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, if I set the seed, I can,
for example, change the prompt.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:16.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I used the same image and
the same settings, and here I say:

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:18.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
I can play around with the style.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I'm going to say: I want it to
be a bit like a futuristic building.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what you get.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:28.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
On the other hand,
it's clear that I'm having a little

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
more trouble with the image's content,

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:34.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
My tango dancers—they're a
little stuck in the QR code.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
They're a little weird.
We'll deal with this later.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Let's focus on the style first,
and then we'll see what we can do

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:44.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
on the content and see if we
can do things a little better.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:45.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay?

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:47.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, when it comes to style,
what I can do is have

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:49.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
some slightly more advanced prompts.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
Same thing here—it's still the same
workflow; I just changed the prompt.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:57.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
So I went to an LLM and
asked it: "Give me a prompt."

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:00.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
A nice way to depict
something a little elven,

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
which is kind of, I don't know, I'm
getting into the DevFest Nantes spirit.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
No? Well, then, it could
take more or less time.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because, in fact, in Stable Diffusion,

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
there are also models that are capable
of understanding text and therefore

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
to transform the idea or
intention behind the image

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:17.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
in this latent space.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:20.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
We can do that, and we
can go even further by

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
adding a custom node called

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:24.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
What's that guy's name?

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:26.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
ITools Prompt Styler Extra.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:27.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
I have to lean over, too.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:30.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
If Thierry were here,
he'd say, "We're old."

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's the green box.

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:35.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
What's interesting here is
that it makes it easy to inject

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:36.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
in the predefined styles prompt.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:41.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, these are text files behind
the scenes with things that have

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
have been predefined and
are organized by category.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
We have lots of categories: 3D,
crafts, design, drawing, painting, etc.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
And it's very easy to inject a style

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:53.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
like this in the command prompt.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, let's modify the
workflow from earlier.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
I just added this box; everything
else is already in place.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:14:59.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay?

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:02.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, as I said, there are lots
of possible categories here.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, I'm going to choose "Crafts."

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:08.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
And when it comes to crafts, well,
you see, there's a whole list.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:09.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know how many there are anymore.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
That makes 400 different styles.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:13.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
And on top of that, the best
part is that you can stack them.

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
So in this case, I stuck with one
style because otherwise, you get stuck.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
There, I set the seed
because it wasn't yet, just

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:19.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
so that we can see the impact.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
And at this point, I start to
speed things up because otherwise,

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:25.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
we're going to wait a little
too long. And then, bam!

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
In that case, I chose Metal Work.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
This has a bit of a metal vibe.

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
There, you can try
scanning—it should work.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:33.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
I've tested it thoroughly
and everything's fine.

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:37.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm changing my style,
so everything else is already set.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:40.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this case, I chose Crochet.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Come to think of it,
maybe it was my grandmother who had...

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:47.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
We can do some cool stuff—like wool
polo shirts with QR codes. Why not?

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:55.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
Still in the Craft category,
by changing just one setting in my

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:57.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
workflow—that's what interests me.

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:00.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
To pay homage to its Japanese origins,
I say "origami" because it

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:01.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
It intrigues me.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Origami, QR codes—I don't
know how that'll turn out.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:06.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
So that's how it turns out.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:09.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
It feels a bit like paper, but with
origami, you're not supposed to cut it.

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:10.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, origami—I don't know.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
And a last one to wrap things up,
because I thought this

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:14.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
one was pretty cool.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:17.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
And besides, it'll give me a clue
as to the true origin of QR codes.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, maybe it was the Romans
who invented QR codes—maybe.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:21.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Come on, it's okay.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, you can see that we're actually
capable of doing some pretty funny things

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:28.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
and it's relatively easy once
you've tweaked your workflow.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I've added some more,
and you can even mix them together.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:35.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Next, you need to have things that
are compatible with one another,

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
otherwise, the styles are
starting to get a little weird.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:37.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay?

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's where you can experiment
with different styles—it's cool.

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:43.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, I'm going to get back to
my issue with the tango dancers.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:47.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
And besides, as Ambre said,
I'm actually a yoga teacher.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:49.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
In my case, I needed QR
codes for things that had

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:50.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
connections to yoga.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
What I want to do now is, in the QR code,

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:55.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
inject a yoga pose.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:57.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Just the posture, by the way.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:59.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
I want it to look like a real
human and all that, but I want

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
that specific posture.
To do that, what do I do?

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:04.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
I also use ControlNets, but this time,

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:06.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
they will extract other types
of information from the image.

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:10.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this case, it's going to
be the depth and the edges.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:11.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Why am I doing this?

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:12.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because I only want to focus on posture.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:16.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
Then, following the prompt, I'll say:
"The guy is dressed like that," etc.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay? So, what's going to happen now?

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:24.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
It'll just take that and then do it again,
with my previous ControlNet

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:24.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
for the QR code.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I have three ControlNets; send
all of that to the latent space.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:31.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
There's a ControlNet called
OpenPose that seemed promising,

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:34.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
but that's not specific enough for a
yoga teacher like me, so it won't work.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:39.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
After trying them out over and over again,
I use these two because

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:40.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
they can be stacked.

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, as you can see,
I'm adding the box at the very bottom.

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here are the nodes I had—the prompts.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:47.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here is the
ControlNet for the QR code.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:52.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
And here, at the very bottom, are
two columns for extracting the posture.

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:57.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I have the first one, where you
can actually see the image I use as input

00:17:57.400 --> 00:17:58.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
the second
input of my workflow.

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:03.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then I choose the type of ControlNet I
want, the type of preprocessor,

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:04.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
because it involves image processing.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:08.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
It will extract that from the image
and send it to the ControlNet above.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
On this ControlNet, I can set the force.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, we can tweak things
a bit. When does it start?

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:16.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
When does it end in
the generation process?

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:18.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, I stack them—I put
two on top of each other.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:22.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, as you can see, the data has
been preprocessed and extracted.

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:26.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go. There's the depth,
there are the contours.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
And all of that is fed into the
generation process, which I'm speeding up.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
And you end up with something like this.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:36.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
What's interesting is that here,
it blended the two together a bit.

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, it's no longer just an image
that's superimposed on top,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:39.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
because there's also

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:41.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
the other ControlNet,
which insists it has to be a QR code.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:43.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it lets you do some
pretty cool things.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:45.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Actually, I think his hair
is a little too white.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
I didn't write "don't include
gray hair" in the negative prompt.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:51.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's where it generates that.

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:55.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
He doesn't have white hair, but he
has a few gray hairs at his temples.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know what I'm trying to do.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
Maybe you've been trying
to guess—I'm trying to do

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:00.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
something that looks just like me.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
Other than that, I really did lie in the prompt,
because I turned 40 a long time ago

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:10.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
but a man who's somewhat
mixed-race, etc., wearing glasses, etc.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:14.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
It turns out that, after all,
I am able to replicate the posture.

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:19.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
It's still scannable, and it works,
but it doesn't really feel like me.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:22.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
What can I do to make it
really reflect who I am?

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:24.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Because I'm a psychopath.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay, I'm going to transfer the face now.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:28.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
How do I do that?

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:32.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I'm using another technique
called Image Prompt Adapter,

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:37.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
IP Adapter, which allows you to
use an image as part of the prompt.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:38.920 line:95% align:center position:50%
With models

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
All of that comes with
pre-trained models, etc.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:44.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
There is a type of IP adapter
called FaceID, which is specialized

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:45.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
in facial recognition.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, he's going to extract the facial
features and incorporate

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:50.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
them into the new generation.

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
Also, by the way, I use a
second type of model as well

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:58.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
called FaceDetailer, which is a
post-generation tool and which is

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:00.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
trained to enhance any face,
whatever it may be,

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:05.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
on the nose, lips, mouth,
facial expressions, etc.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:08.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm going to combine all of
this into my final workflow.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:11.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
Those are the parts in
yellow that I added there.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:16.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
As you can see, we still have the
elements from our previous workflow.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:19.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Right here at the top, I have the
section on Face ID and the IP Adapter.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:21.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
That will be sent, and it will
generate an initial image.

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then the result of this first
generation will be sent down,

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:27.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
in FaceDetailer to generate
a second, even better image.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:29.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Okay, so let's get things started.

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:33.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And while it's rolling,
I zoom in on that part,

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:35.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
Face ID, just so you can see.

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:37.240 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this case, it comes
with a lot of settings.

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:40.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's what's a little crazy—we
actually have a lot of control.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here's the image I'm using as the starter.

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:46.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
In this case, I'll choose one,
because there are several

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:47.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
versions of Face ID, etc.

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:50.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, I'm choosing V2—what's
the strength, and then,

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:53.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
There are also quite a few
settings you can tweak here.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:55.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go—that starts the generation;
the rest is just like before.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, we have the first image that's been
generated—it looks just

00:20:58.280 --> 00:20:59.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
like me, in every way

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:01.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
a little more than a moment ago.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And here, as you can see,
it's been processed through FaceDetailer.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:04.960 line:95% align:center position:50%
We're in the second half.

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:07.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
You see, FaceDetailer
is linked to Face ID.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, it's going to get better.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:11.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I have a node that comes
before and after FaceDetailer.

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:13.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
If I zoom in, maybe
you'll be able to see it.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's better. I'm younger.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:17.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
AI always makes us look even better.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:20.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
I don't know if you've noticed. Anyway,
we're capable of doing that.

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:22.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
That's nice. What did I do?

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:25.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
There, I managed to transfer the QR
code using an initial ControlNet,

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
the installation,
along with two other ControlNets,

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:35.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then the face—so there,
with IP Adapter. That's cool.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:37.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Of course, you can mix
all of that together.

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:39.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
I can come back to the styles.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:40.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
Now, if I put the

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:42.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
I won't go through the demo with you
anymore—we won't have time for it anyway,

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:46.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
But if I put the green box back in,
we'll be able to mix

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.880 line:95% align:center position:50%
and therefore to change the styles.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:49.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
This is starting to get really fun.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:53.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then, just to top it off,
since I'm even more of a psychopath

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
Believe it or not, I said,
“I’d like to bring the QR code to life.”

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:57.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
I want to create animated QR codes.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:00.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
Personally, I'd like the
face in the QR code to move.

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:05.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
For that, I use Advanced Live Portrait,
which is a specialized model

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:06.480 line:95% align:center position:50%
in facial expressions.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:09.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
He draws them out, knows how to bring them
to life, and then chain them together.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:11.200 line:95% align:center position:50%
It works something like this.

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:14.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
That means we have an editor,
and it's preconfigured.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:19.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
You can make it turn its head,
raise its eyebrows—that sort of thing.

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:23.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here, I'll show you
another workflow I created.

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:26.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
I could have chained him to the other one,
but I preferred to keep them apart.

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:29.440 line:95% align:center position:50%
I have an image that I had already
generated using my first workflow.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.760 line:95% align:center position:50%
Here's my expression editor.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
So I tell him which
step I want him to take.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:37.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
The third node—there's
a bit of formalism here—

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:40.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
I won't go into detail,
but it explains how to get there,

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:43.360 line:95% align:center position:50%
How many installments,
how long does it take, etc.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
And then the last node will
combine all of that to create

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:48.720 line:95% align:center position:50%
a video, because a video
is a sequence of images.

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
So, the frame rate is
important—it's the number of frames

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:53.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
per second that it will use.

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:55.280 line:95% align:center position:50%
And besides, all of that
generates something.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'm going to skip that part because we
don't have much time left, but at the

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:00.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
In the end, it looks something like this.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:02.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
As you can see, I could tweak it a bit.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:05.520 line:95% align:center position:50%
Guys who are good at observing,
who have an eagle's eye,

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.040 line:95% align:center position:50%
will see that part of the QR
code near the forehead moves.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:10.320 line:95% align:center position:50%
I'll have to tweak things a little bit.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.120 line:95% align:center position:50%
This works; it scans.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.160 line:95% align:center position:50%
With the iPhone, you can scan.

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:17.400 line:95% align:center position:50%
But the iPhone sometimes tries
to be really clever when it

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:19.560 line:95% align:center position:50%
He takes photos, and he
also shoots videos, etc.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:20.680 line:95% align:center position:50%
So it depends on when.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:23.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
But if you install an iPhone app,
it usually works, too.

00:23:24.520 --> 00:23:24.840 line:95% align:center position:50%
There you go.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:28.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
So we're able to do that kind
of thing relatively easily

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:29.800 line:95% align:center position:50%
locally, using open-source tools.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.080 line:95% align:center position:50%
I think it's fun.

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.000 line:95% align:center position:50%
So I wanted to share this with you.

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:34.600 line:95% align:center position:50%
Thank you very much.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:37.640 line:95% align:center position:50%
That QR code right there wasn't
